  "description": "Resize 2D spatial image matrix to designated dimensions using bilinear four-point coordinate interpolation.\n\nBilinear interpolation reconstructs continuous 2D surface patches using separable 1D linear interpolations along horizontal and vertical axes.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\nf(x, y) \\approx [1 - \\Delta x, \\Delta x] \\begin{bmatrix} f(Q_{11}) & f(Q_{12}) \\\\ f(Q_{21}) & f(Q_{22}) \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} 1 - \\Delta y \\\\ \\Delta y \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Map target coordinate grid into continuous source coordinate space with half-pixel alignment\n2. Identify bounding 2x2 integer pixel neighborhood [y0, y1] x [x0, x1]\n3. Linearly interpolate horizontally and vertically across neighborhood and round to 4 decimal places\n\n### Returns\nNested 2D list of shape (new_h, new_w) representing resized image.",
  "theory": "## What Bilinear Resize Does\n\nResize 2D spatial image matrix to designated dimensions using bilinear four-point coordinate interpolation. Bilinear interpolation reconstructs continuous 2D surface patches using separable 1D linear interpolations along horizontal and vertical axes. 1. Map target coordinate grid into continuous source coordinate space with half-pixel alignment 2. Identify bounding 2x2 integer pixel neighborhood [y0, y1] x [x0, x1] 3. Linearly interpolate horizontally and vertically across neighborhood and round to 4 decimal places Nested 2D list of shape (new_h, new_w) representing resized image.\n\nAt its core, this problem asks you to implement `cv_bilinear_resize` to perform this computation while maintaining numerical precision, handling dimensional variations, and ensuring optimal performance.\n\n---\n\n## Core Intuition & Visual Walkthrough\n\nVectorized numerical computing transforms complex mathematical formulations into high-performance array operations.\n\n- **Vectorized SIMD Operations**: Modern CPU and GPU hardware executes operations across entire array buffers simultaneously rather than iterating over individual elements.\n- **Broadcasting Dimensions**: Dimension alignment rules allow arrays with different but compatible shapes to operate together seamlessly without redundant data copying.\n- **Mathematical Invariants**: Adhering strictly to dimensional specifications guarantees consistent behavior across single samples and batched inputs.\n\n**Concrete Example Walkthrough:**\n```text\nInput: image = [[1.0, 2.0], [3.0, 4.0]], new_h = 3, new_w = 3\nOutput: [[1.0, 1.5, 2.0], [2.0, 2.5, 3.0], [3.0, 3.5, 4.0]]\nExplanation: Evaluated cv_bilinear_resize under standard test conditions.\n```\n\n---\n\n## The Mathematical Formulation\n\nThe core computation is governed by the following mathematical formulation:\n\n$$\nf(x, y) \\approx [1 - \\Delta x, \\Delta x] \\begin{bmatrix} f(Q_{11}) & f(Q_{12}) \\\\ f(Q_{21}) & f(Q_{22}) \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} 1 - \\Delta y \\\\ \\Delta y \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Parameters & Dimensional Roles:**\n- `$image` (list): Input parameter specifying features, targets, or operational hyperparameters.\n- `$new_h` (int): Input parameter specifying features, targets, or operational hyperparameters.\n- `$new_w` (int): Input parameter specifying features, targets, or operational hyperparameters.\n- **Output**: Resulting tensor or scalar matching the mathematical transformation.\n\n---\n\n## Step-by-Step Implementation Guide\n\nTo implement `cv_bilinear_resize` correctly, follow these structured steps:\n\n1. **Input Preparation & Shape Inspection**:\n   Convert incoming inputs into standard NumPy arrays using `np.asarray(...)` to handle lists, tuples, and tensors uniformly. Inspect `.shape` and verify dimensional constraints.\n\n2. **Vectorized Core Transformation**:\n   Implement the mathematical formulation using vectorized array operations. Avoid slow Python `for` loops across sample or feature dimensions by taking advantage of NumPy broadcasting.\n\n3. **Reduction & Axis Management**:\n   When computing reductions (such as sums, means, variances, or dot products), specify the exact `axis` parameter (typically `axis=-1` for feature-level operations) and use `keepdims=True` if downstream dimension alignment is required.\n\n4. **Return Value Formatting**:\n   Ensure the final returned object strictly matches the expected output signature (e.g. scalar float, integer, tuple, or NumPy array).\n\n**Key Requirements Checklist:**\n* Function must be named `cv_bilinear_resize`\n* Return value must match the exact expected structure and types\n* Handle edge cases cleanly and maintain numerical stability\n* Must run efficiently without redundant memory allocations\n\n---\n\n## Practical Considerations & Numerical Stability\n\nWhen deploying `cv_bilinear_resize` in machine learning workflows:\n\n- **Numerical Stability**: Safeguard against division by zero and log underflow by adding a tiny epsilon constant (e.g. $\\epsilon = 10^{-8}$) to denominators or inside `np.log(...)`.\n- **Broadcasting & Shapes**: Verify that 1D vectors and 2D batch matrices are handled gracefully. Use `.reshape(-1, 1)` or `np.expand_dims(...)` to align singleton dimensions when necessary.\n- **Memory Contiguity**: Prefer in-place operations or contiguous memory buffers (`np.ascontiguousarray`) to ensure maximum CPU cache line utilization and SIMD execution speed.\n",
