  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nThe implementation computes **Bilinear Resize** by following direct mathematical principles, leveraging vectorized operations to ensure both correctness and optimal runtime efficiency.\n\n### ⚡ Python Reference Implementation\n```python\nimport numpy as np, math\n\ndef cv_bilinear_resize(image: list, new_h: int, new_w: int) -> list:\n    img = np.array(image, dtype=float)\n    H, W = img.shape; out = np.zeros((new_h, new_w))\n    sy, sx = H / new_h, W / new_w\n    for i in range(new_h):\n        for j in range(new_w):\n            src_y, src_x = (i + 0.5) * sy - 0.5, (j + 0.5) * sx - 0.5\n            y0, x0 = max(0, int(math.floor(src_y))), max(0, int(math.floor(src_x)))\n            y1, x1 = min(y0 + 1, H - 1), min(x0 + 1, W - 1)\n            dy, dx = src_y - math.floor(src_y), src_x - math.floor(src_x)\n            t = (1.0 - dx) * img[y0, x0] + dx * img[y0, x1]\n            b = (1.0 - dx) * img[y1, x0] + dx * img[y1, x1]\n            out[i, j] = (1.0 - dy) * t + dy * b\n    return np.round(out, 4).tolist()\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the size of the primary input.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ auxiliary memory for computing and storing output representations.",
